+Marcus had trained in forests, but he had never felt at home in them. Most of his work with the Army Corps of Engineers had been in the deserts of Africa, designing irrigation systems for remote settlements. He missed the desert. He missed knowing what needed to be done.
+
+He sat a moment longer, waiting for his muscles to recover. His knack for solving problems others had abandoned had brought him to the ARC project. He could still remember the briefings: the machine beneath the ice, the discovery of what it was built to do, the panic that followed.
+
+They had learned enough from the weapon to escape it. Or so they had hoped.
+
+“Here I am.” His voice still sounded like gravel.
+
+He got unsteadily to his feet and shuffled around the pod. The hull was buckled. It would never fly again. Looking up through the branches, he imagined the mothership in orbit, its remaining pods waiting in their berths for the artificial intelligence to release them.
+
+Something large moved in the woods. Marcus turned toward the sound, then edged around to the rear of the pod.
+
+He was a soldier, but he had spent his career building things. What little he knew of fighting came from basic training.
+
+The rear compartment appeared undamaged. He listened, hearing nothing more from the trees, and found the recessed panel beneath his fingers. A keypad slid out. He entered his code and waited until the storage door opened.
+
+He unloaded a backpack, tent, food, and bottled water, stacking everything beside the hull. At the back of the compartment, his hand closed on a black plastic case. Inside lay a military-issue carbine and pistol, with spare magazines nestled in the padding.
+
+He loaded the carbine and pulled back the charging handle. The metallic clack of a round entering the chamber reassured him. He moved around the pod to face the woods.
+
+After a moment, he aimed at a broad tree trunk and squeezed the trigger. A warning shot might drive off whatever was out there.
+
+Click.
+
+He lifted his cheek from the stock. He ejected the round, chambered another, and tried again.
+
+Another click.
+
+“What the hell?” At least his voice was beginning to sound like his own.
+
+He removed the magazine and searched his supplies for a tool case. When he opened one of the cartridges, a few taps freed a solid yellowish clump from the casing.
+
+“Of all the stupid…”
+
+The propellant had deteriorated. He closed his eyes, uncertain whether to laugh or swear. They had kept him alive for centuries, but no one had managed the same for his ammunition.
+
+He put the carbine back in its case and searched the remaining equipment. A knife and a survival hatchet would have to do. He weighed the knife in his hand.
+
+If it’s as big as it sounded, this will just piss it off.
+
+Still, it was better than nothing.
+
+He scanned the trees, chose a long, straight branch, and cut it free with the hatchet. Working the end to a point gave him something useful to concentrate on. He tested the tip with a finger, then leaned the makeshift spear against a tree within reach.
+
+Next came the sticky one-piece suit. He stripped it off and wiped away as much of the reanimation fluid as he could with a spare T-shirt. Fresh underwear, socks, and military fatigues followed. The cloth caught against his skin, but being dressed again helped him feel less like something newly hatched.
+
+He took a deep breath of forest air. Whatever else had happened, the world was alive. He might have returned to a desert, or to a planet with nothing left on it at all.
+
+The machine had been ancient when they found it beneath the Arctic ice. For fifty years it had sat dormant in a research facility. Then an experiment had activated it. A year passed before the scientists understood its purpose—and learned to read the countdown.
+
+Six years. They had already wasted one.
+
+The weapon would release pathogens and nanite swarms to erase humanity and its works. With five years remaining, the ARC project had begun adapting what the researchers had learned: ships, cryonic systems, a way to wait out the destruction.
+
+Marcus looked around. There should have been other pods. Other people.
+
+He reached into the pod’s control assembly and removed a chrome device the size and shape of an egg. Set on the ground, it pulsed red, then yellow. When the light turned green, the device rose to eye level. A wireframe figure formed around it and filled out into a dark silver human silhouette.
+
+“Sentinel is online, Marcus Gavin. By your command.”
+
+He let out a breath. The virtual interface worked, at least. Sentinel had no independent intelligence; it connected him to the ship’s AI. If anyone else had returned, perhaps the ship could help him find them.
+
+“Sentinel, review the mission and bring me up to speed.”
+
+The figure froze while it contacted the ship.
+
+“Certainly, Marcus.”
+
+The silhouette dissolved into an image of Earth. City lights gleamed on the night side. An archive recording, he knew, but the sight caught in his throat.
+
+“Beginning time lapse.”
+
+Pods rose from the surface and docked with the mothership. He had been aboard one of them, frozen before launch and loaded into a berth like cargo.
+
+The launches stopped. The image accelerated.
+
+One by one, the lights went out. A red haze spread across the continents, swallowing coastlines and mountains until he could see nothing beneath it. Marcus watched without speaking. Eventually, the haze thinned and vanished.
+
+The recording ran on. At last it slowed, and pods streamed from the mothership toward Earth. Two more ships came into view and released their own passengers. Then the image accelerated again.
+
+At intervals, a lone pod descended. Marcus counted twelve scattered launches before the final one. As that pod fell toward the surface, the recording stopped.
+
+“Sentinel, what was the red dust?”
+
+The Earth vanished. The silver figure returned.
+
+“Our sensors identified a swarm of nanite machines. They deconstructed artificial materials on the surface. The swarm eventually became inactive, leaving large crystal formations behind. Those formations emit a signal that interferes with our observations of the surface.”
+
+Marcus looked around. Red, blue, and green crystals studded the trees and soil. He bent over a small spire and touched it, then jerked his hand away. Slowly, he tried again. The crystal thrummed beneath his fingertip.
+
+He straightened. Once he knew what to look for, he could see them everywhere.
+
+“Are they generators? Communication equipment?”
+
+“They emit power, although the source is unknown. They also produce a constant, low-level signal. Its effective range appears to be about a thousand feet, insufficient to reach orbit. We have detected no information in it. The original spires have broken down over time; their remnants are now part of the soil and vegetation. They appear to coexist with the surrounding life.”
+
+Interesting, but it would not help him find the other volunteers.
+
+“Why were pods still launching after the main return?”
+
+“Thirty pods remained aboard because of a malfunction. As repairs were completed, they were released. Twelve have returned over the two centuries since the main landing.”
+
+“Two centuries to fix twelve pods? What was wrong with them?”
+
+The pause was longer this time. When Sentinel moved again, she shrugged. The human gesture unsettled him.
+
+“That information requires administrator access.”
+
+Marcus narrowed his eyes. “Who restricted it?”
+
+“That information requires administrator access.”
+
+He shook his head. Something was wrong, but the next question mattered more.
+
